<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Sorry, I Can't Help You. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Sorry, I Can't Help You.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="4" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain simple rules of a game or activity. Interactive: Collaborate verbally in team activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can politely explain the rules of a simple game (e.g., "Please roll the dice and move your piece."). Can collaborate with peers in a group activity, sharing ideas and listening to others (e.g., "Good idea. Let's use green!"). Can describe the inability to do something in a game or activity in a polite manner (e.g., "I'm sorry. I can't roll the dice.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using actions related to classroom objects and polite requests/inability. For example: "Simon says, 'Can I have a pencil, please?'" (students mime taking a pencil), "Simon says, 'I can't draw today.'" (students mime sadness and shaking head). This activates prior vocabulary and introduces polite expressions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models target language and pronunciation clearly. The teacher picks up a pencil and says, "Can I have a paper, please?" then picks up paper and says, "Here you are. My pleasure." Then, the teacher tries to pick up another pencil but finds none and says, "I'm sorry, I can't help you. I don't have another pencil." The teacher exaggerates the /p/ sound in "please" and the /k/ sound in "can't."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with simple questions and examples.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want a pencil, do I say 'Give me a pencil' or 'Can I have a pencil, please'?" (Expected answer: 'Can I have a pencil, please.')</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I don't have an eraser to give my friend, what do I say?" (Expected answer: 'I'm sorry, I can't help you.' or 'I don't have an eraser.')</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Polite Request".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue projected on the board or printed on flashcards. The dialogue features two friends, Leo and Mia, in a classroom setting, asking for and offering/declining school supplies or help with a simple task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
                    Leo: "Mia, can I borrow your pencil, please?"<br>
                    Mia: "Sure, Leo! Here you are. My pleasure."<br>
                    Leo: "Thank you!"<br>
                    Mia: "Leo, can you help me with this drawing? I need a green crayon."<br>
                    Leo: "I'm sorry, Mia. I can't help you. I don't have a green crayon. I only have blue."<br>
                    Mia: "Oh, okay. No problem."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing inability politely using "can't" in respectful conversation and making polite requests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, pointing to illustrations. Students listen and then answer questions orally.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Leo and Mia talking about?" (Expected: School things, helping each other.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Choral response):</b>
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo has a green crayon." (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Mia gives Leo a pencil." (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo can help Mia with the green crayon." (False)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Making polite requests ("Can I have/borrow...please?", "Can you help me?"). Expressing polite inability ("I'm sorry, I can't help you. I don't have..."). Using target vocabulary (pencil, paper, desk, friend, help, turn, roll, ask, need, know) and the contracted form of 'can't'. Pronunciation practice for /p/ and /k/, and blending phonemes for words like "kind."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Fill in the Blank" (Oral)</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks and offers two word choices orally. Students choose and say the correct word to complete the polite request or refusal.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I ____ your pencil, please? (borrow / take)"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I'm sorry, I ____ help you. (can / can't)"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Please ____ the dice. (roll / give)"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you ____ me an answer, please? (ask / give)"</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Sentence Chain" (Pair work)</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives a sentence starter: "Can I have a ____, please?" Students then take turns substituting nouns from a list on the board or flashcards (e.g., *pencil, paper, turn, hand*). The partner responds "Yes, you can. Here you are." or "I'm sorry, I can't help you. I don't have a ____." The teacher circulates, correcting pronunciation (especially /p/ in please and /k/ in can't) and intonation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Pair Interaction: "Asking and Saying No Politely"</b> Students are given a set of picture cards (e.g., a pencil, a desk, a hand, dice, a friend, an empty bag). In pairs, one student asks a polite request using a card (e.g., "Can I borrow your pencil, please?"), and the other student uses another card to either accept ("Yes, you can. Here you are!") or politely refuse ("I'm sorry, I can't help you. I don't have a pencil.") They swap roles and cards. Emphasize speaking clearly and using polite expressions like "please" and "thank you."</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Polite Request".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Play a Simple Board Game" Role-Play</b> Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a simple board game (e.g., "Path to the Treasure") with spaces like "Roll the dice," "Move 2 spaces," "Lose a turn," "Ask a friend for help." The goal is for students to play the game while practicing polite requests, offering help, and politely expressing inability.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains the basic rules of the simple game (e.g., "Roll the dice," "Move your piece," "Take a turn").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns playing. When it's their turn, they say, "Can I roll the dice, please?" or "Can I move my piece?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If a player lands on a "Help" space, they can say, "Can you help me move my piece, please?" or "Do you need help?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If a player cannot perform an action (e.g., "Lose a turn" but they don't want to, or if a friend asks for something they don't have), they must politely say, "I'm sorry, I can't roll the dice, it's not my turn," or "I'm sorry, I can't help you, I don't have a blue piece."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use phrases like "Good idea," "Please," "Thank you," and "My pleasure."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes and provides support as needed, focusing on fluent communication rather than perfect accuracy.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe each group's use of target language during the game. This allows for real-time feedback and tracking of student progress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Play a Simple Board Game" role-play, actively making polite requests, offering help, and politely refusing/explaining inability.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "Can I have/borrow...please?" for polite requests.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "I'm sorry, I can't help you. I don't have..." to express inability politely.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't" is generally clear.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates verbally and respectfully with peers in the group activity.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can explain simple game actions (e.g., "roll the dice," "move your piece").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students orally complete 1-2 reflective prompts before leaving the classroom.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I ask my friends politely for things today?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to say 'I'm sorry, I can't help'?" (Easy/Difficult)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (oral or written):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new polite sentence you can say?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you be a more helpful and friendly friend in English?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., The board game activity encouraged natural interaction and use of target phrases. Students enjoyed the role-play and were keen to use "Can I have...please?" and "I'm sorry, I can't.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (Some students struggled with the pronunciation of /k/ in 'can't' – more focused drills might be useful. For the next lesson (Writing), I will provide sentence starters and visual prompts to help students write polite requests and refusals effectively, building on their oral practice today.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson provided ample oral practice for making polite requests and expressing inability. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will transition this oral practice into written form, where students will compose simple sentences expressing the same functions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>